  "slides": [
    {
      "page_title": "What a Transaction Actually Is",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "A __transaction__ is a ==unit of work== that moves the database from one __consistent state__ to another — **all-or-nothing**. Half of it never survives.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Boundaries",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "BEGIN;  -- or START TRANSACTION",
            "  UPDATE ...;",
            "COMMIT;    -- make durable",
            "ROLLBACK;  -- undo everything"
          ],
          "note": "autocommit: each statement is one txn"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "{blue:Postgres} / {orange:MySQL}: __autocommit ON__ by default",
            "{red:SQL Server}: needs `BEGIN TRAN` explicitly",
            "Or `SET IMPLICIT_TRANSACTIONS ON` per session",
            "No `BEGIN` = every statement commits alone"
          ],
          "style": "dot"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. The Canonical Example",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "BEGIN;",
            "UPDATE acct SET bal=bal-100",
            "  WHERE id='A';",
            "UPDATE acct SET bal=bal+100",
            "  WHERE id='B';",
            "COMMIT;  -- both, or neither"
          ],
          "note": "crash between = money vanishes"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Savepoints",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "SAVEPOINT sp1;",
            "INSERT ...;  -- oops",
            "ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT sp1;",
            "RELEASE SAVEPOINT sp1;"
          ],
          "note": "partial undo, outer txn stays alive"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Transaction States",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              {
                "text": "active",
                "sub": "running"
              },
              {
                "text": "partially committed",
                "sub": "last stmt done"
              },
              {
                "text": "committed",
                "sub": "durable",
                "ink": "green"
              },
              {
                "text": "failed",
                "sub": "error",
                "ink": "red"
              },
              {
                "text": "aborted",
                "sub": "rolled back",
                "ink": "red"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Four __guarantees__ a transaction buys you: ==Atomicity==, ==Consistency==, ==Isolation==, ==Durability== — only one of them has a dial you can turn.",
          "ink": "blue"
        }
      ],
      "hero": "postgresql",
      "subtitle": "Unit of work"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "DDL, Read-Only Txns & ID Gaps",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. DDL Is Not Portable",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Engine",
            "DDL in txn",
            "Effect"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Postgres",
              "transactional",
              "`CREATE TABLE` rolls back"
            ],
            [
              "MySQL",
              "implicit commit",
              "`ALTER` **ends** your txn"
            ],
            [
              "Oracle",
              "implicit commit",
              "commits work silently"
            ]
          ],
          "frame": "dashed"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. Read-Only Transactions",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`SET TRANSACTION READ ONLY` before first read",
            "Engine skips ==undo/rollback segment== setup",
            "No XID assigned until a write happens",
            "Cheap snapshot for long reports"
          ],
          "style": "dot"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "ID gaps are normal",
          "text": "A **rolled-back** txn still burns sequence / AUTO_INCREMENT values — counters are non-transactional so they never block. Gaps in IDs are not a bug."
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Never assume IDs are gapless or contiguous",
            "A mid-txn `ALTER` in MySQL silently commits",
            "`ROLLBACK TO sp1` does __not__ end the transaction"
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Summary",
          "text": "Mark boundaries yourself; know your engine's autocommit and DDL rules. Next: ==ACID== letter by letter."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. The Four Letters",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Letter",
            "Promise",
            "Machinery"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "**A**tomicity",
              "all or none",
              "undo log / rollback seg"
            ],
            [
              "**C**onsistency",
              "valid state → valid",
              "constraints, FK, triggers"
            ],
            [
              "**I**solation",
              "no partial work seen",
              "MVCC + locks (tunable)"
            ],
            [
              "**D**urability",
              "survives crash",
              "WAL + `fsync`"
            ]
          ],
          "frame": "solid"
        }
      ],
      "hero": "postgresql",
      "subtitle": "Unit of work"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "ACID, One Letter at a Time",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Atomicity: a `ROLLBACK` walks the ==undo log== backward, undoing each row change.",
            "Consistency is mostly __your__ job: the engine only enforces what the schema declares.",
            "Isolation is the **only** letter with a knob: `SET TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL ...` (page 4).",
            "Durability: once `COMMIT` returns, the change is on stable storage — not in RAM."
          ],
          "style": "number",
          "ink": "black"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Mechanism: the WAL rule",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "==Write-Ahead Logging==: the __log record must reach stable storage before the data page does__. Log first, page later.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              {
                "text": "change row",
                "sub": "in buffer pool"
              },
              {
                "text": "WAL record",
                "sub": "fsync to disk",
                "ink": "red"
              },
              {
                "text": "COMMIT returns",
                "sub": "client unblocked",
                "ink": "green"
              },
              {
                "text": "data page",
                "sub": "flushed later"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. The durability knobs",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Setting",
            "Value",
            "On crash you lose"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "PG `synchronous_commit`",
              "`on`",
              "nothing"
            ],
            [
              "PG `synchronous_commit`",
              "`off`",
              "~3× wal_writer_delay"
            ],
            [
              "`innodb_flush_log_at_trx_commit`",
              "`1`",
              "nothing (default)"
            ],
            [
              "`innodb_flush_log_at_trx_commit`",
              "`2`",
              "only on OS crash"
            ],
            [
              "`innodb_flush_log_at_trx_commit`",
              "`0`",
              "~1s of commits"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 2,
          "frame": "solid"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "SET synchronous_commit = off;",
            "-- fast bulk load, then:",
            "SET synchronous_commit = on;",
            "-- MySQL: SET GLOBAL",
            "-- innodb_flush_log_at_trx_commit=2;"
          ],
          "note": "off = durability traded for throughput"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Example: crash after COMMIT",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Example : `UPDATE accounts SET bal=900 WHERE id=1;` `COMMIT;` returns, then the machine loses power **before** the dirty page is written.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "pink"
        }
      ],
      "hero": "postgresql",
      "subtitle": "The four guarantees"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Crash Recovery, BASE & Write Skew",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Restart: engine reads the WAL from the last ==checkpoint==.",
            "{green:Redo} — replay committed records, reapplying `bal=900`.",
            "{red:Undo} — roll back records from transactions with no commit record.",
            "Page on disk was stale; the log made the commit **survive**."
          ],
          "style": "number",
          "ink": "black"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Comparison: ACID vs BASE",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "",
            "ACID",
            "BASE"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Scope",
              "one node / cluster",
              "distributed store"
            ],
            [
              "Reads",
              "always current",
              "==eventually== consistent"
            ],
            [
              "Cost",
              "cross-node consensus",
              "cheap, always writable"
            ],
            [
              "Example",
              "Postgres, InnoDB",
              "Dynamo, Cassandra"
            ]
          ],
          "frame": "solid"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Distributed stores relax the letters because a commit needs a __network round trip__ to every replica.",
            "BASE = **B**asically **A**vailable, **S**oft state, **E**ventual consistency."
          ],
          "style": "dot",
          "ink": "black"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Key rule",
          "text": "Log before page, `fsync` before `COMMIT` returns. Every durability knob is just a decision about __when to skip that fsync__."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "__Anomalies__ are the wrong answers concurrency produces when two transactions ==interleave==. Each stronger isolation level buys the removal of one more.",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Example : T1 acted on a balance of {red:900} that no committed state ever held. Real bal is `1000`.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "pink"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Write Skew",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "-- both docs run this",
            "SELECT count(*) FROM shifts",
            "  WHERE on_call = true; -- 2, ok",
            "UPDATE shifts SET on_call=false",
            "  WHERE doc = :me; -- disjoint rows!"
          ],
          "note": "invariant: >= 1 doctor on call"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Each transaction wrote a **different** row, so no write conflict fires — yet ==zero doctors== end up on call.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "pink"
        }
      ],
      "hero": "postgresql",
      "subtitle": "The four guarantees"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "The Six Concurrency Anomalies",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. Common Mistakes",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "ACID **C** ≠ CAP **C**: ACID C = schema validity; CAP C = all replicas read the same value.",
            "Thinking Consistency is automatic — no `CHECK`/FK declared means nothing is enforced.",
            "Assuming `COMMIT` returning means durable while `innodb_flush_log_at_trx_commit=0`.",
            "Confusing Atomicity with Isolation: rollback safety is not concurrency safety."
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. The Six Anomalies",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "**Dirty read**: read a row {red:T2} wrote but never committed",
            "**Non-repeatable read**: same row read twice, ==UPDATE== between",
            "**Phantom read**: same range read twice, ==INSERT== between",
            "**Lost update**: two read-modify-writes, second silently wins",
            "**Read skew**: read X, then Y — pair mutually inconsistent"
          ],
          "style": "number",
          "ink": "black"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Dirty Read Walkthrough",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "#",
            "T1",
            "T2",
            "T1 sees"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "1",
              "",
              "`UPDATE bal=900`",
              "—"
            ],
            [
              "2",
              "`SELECT bal`",
              "(uncommitted)",
              "**900**"
            ],
            [
              "3",
              "",
              "`ROLLBACK`",
              "—"
            ],
            [
              "4",
              "emails user",
              "",
              "900 never existed"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 3,
          "frame": "solid"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Lost Update vs Read Skew",
          "ink": "orange"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Anomaly",
            "Reads",
            "Writes",
            "Damage"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Lost update",
              "same row",
              "same row",
              "one write vanishes"
            ],
            [
              "Write skew",
              "a set",
              "disjoint",
              "invariant broken"
            ],
            [
              "Read skew",
              "X then Y",
              "none",
              "inconsistent pair"
            ]
          ],
          "frame": "solid"
        }
      ],
      "hero": "postgresql",
      "subtitle": "What isolation must stop"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "The Four Standard Isolation Levels",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. The Hierarchy",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "chain",
          "spec": {
            "title": "Each level peels one off the top",
            "steps": [
              {
                "text": "READ UNCOMMITTED",
                "note": "allows all",
                "ink": "red"
              },
              {
                "text": "READ COMMITTED",
                "note": "kills dirty read",
                "ink": "orange"
              },
              {
                "text": "REPEATABLE READ",
                "note": "kills non-repeatable",
                "ink": "blue"
              },
              {
                "text": "SERIALIZABLE",
                "note": "kills phantom + skew",
                "ink": "green"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Lost update and skew are **not** in the ANSI table (page 4)",
            "Snapshot isolation still permits ==write skew==",
            "Only true serializability stops every one"
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Interleaving, not speed",
          "text": "A slow query is not **more likely** to be wrong — it is only exposed for __longer__. Shorten transactions to shrink the window."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "The __ANSI standard__ names four levels by ==which anomalies each forbids== — never by how the engine gets there, so two databases at the same level can behave differently.",
          "ink": "black"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. The Four Levels",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "==READ UNCOMMITTED==: **dirty reads** allowed; almost never useful",
            "==READ COMMITTED==: statement sees data committed as of __statement start__",
            "==REPEATABLE READ==: one snapshot as of __transaction start__",
            "==SERIALIZABLE==: result equals **some serial ordering** of the transactions"
          ],
          "style": "number",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Example : Postgres accepts `READ UNCOMMITTED` but silently runs it as {red:READ COMMITTED} — it has no dirty-read code path at all.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "pink"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Setting the Level",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "BEGIN;",
            "SET TRANSACTION ISOLATION",
            "  LEVEL REPEATABLE READ;",
            "-- next txn only",
            "SET SESSION TRANSACTION",
            "  ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE;"
          ],
          "note": "per-txn form must follow BEGIN"
        }
      ],
      "hero": "postgresql",
      "subtitle": "ANSI dial, engine reality"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Same Level Name, Different Engine",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. ANSI Anomaly Table",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Level",
            "Dirty",
            "Non-rep",
            "Phantom"
          ],
          "rows": [
